From 970c8cf6724c08601530700eaf90488a12adfc0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 12:40:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Factory reset: cut the bounded waits, latch the page's
 down-detect, tell the operator how to get back

Timing pass over the GUI factory-reset chain (httpd -> rc -> init),
traced 2026-09-06 for the "took a long time to reboot" report on v3.1.0.

httpd/web.c
- prepare_restore(): the stop_app round trip is a synchronous rc_service
  wait (up to 2 min for rc idle + up to 2 min for completion) and rc's
  stop_app() returns -1 at once unless a USB application is mounted
  (apps_dev + apps_mounted_path). Send it only when it has work.
- both Restore branches: notify_rc_and_wait_2min() instead of the
  sys_default() macro, whose notify_rc() drops the event after 15 s if rc
  is busy (ejusb hotplug churn) and the reset silently never happens.
- "factory reset:" syslog lines at request and at the stop_app decision.

rc/services.c factory_reset()
- take ACT_REBOOT (after wait_action_idle) before stop_wan(), the way the
  "reboot" handler does: later wait_action_idle() callers stop at once
  and shutdn() skips its 30-s busy loop on a stale action lock.
- three syslog timers (begin / wan+usb stopped / erased, handing off).

www/Reaper_Backup.asp + 25 dicts (RBKP_50, RBKP_51)
- the reset veil now says what happens (factory Wi-Fi ASUS_xx, open;
  default LAN IP) with a link to the default address, instead of a
  silent 15-minute poll.
- pollBack latches "down" only after 5 consecutive misses: httpd blocks a
  few seconds preparing the reset and answers again before init takes it
  down, which bounced the page to the login of a router about to reboot.
- if the router never goes down within 3 min, release the page and say
  the reset was not accepted (was: veil until the 15-min cap).

Static check factory-reset-fast (build-scripts, lean) pins all three files.
Metal owed: reset on the BE96U, then read the "factory reset:" lines and
stop_services/REBOOT timestamps from /jffs/syslog.log.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
